    "reference_server": "MySQL 8.4.10",
    "about": "MySQL's STATIC privileges, and the grant-table column each one is stored in. MySQL keeps them as one Y/N column per privilege in `mysql.user` (global scope) and `mysql.db` (database scope), and the column name is not the privilege name — `Show_db_priv` is `SHOW DATABASES`, `Repl_slave_priv` is `REPLICATION SLAVE`. A reader has to carry that mapping somewhere, and carrying it inside a query is how it rots: the first version of this reader unpivoted ten of `mysql.db`'s eighteen privilege columns and none of `mysql.user`'s thirty, so `GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON *.*` reached the rules as nothing at all and looked exactly like an account with no privileges. Here the mapping is a fact with a reference server, reconciled by a test that asks a REAL MySQL for its column list and then asks it to accept every name — the two questions that, together, catch both a column nobody registered and a name nobody can grant.",
